Z||000053||Formula88||08-11-2001||02:19 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by c m west:<BR><B>the dual throttle body has yet to see the light of day ( still in the parts cabinet ), however the 3.4 Dual Over Head Cam throttle body'ed Fiero Intake is a perfect candidate for a 3.4 'maro motor as it will flow the needed air for the engine - highest bidder gets it, with installation and fuel management if installed here at the shop</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>So I take it the 3.4 TDC throttle bodied Fiero Intake is still in the prototype phase?  That was my question.  Basically, how's development coming, and when will it be regularly available.  I'm also looking forward to seeing the twin throttle body intake.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000054||California Kid||08-11-2001||03:01 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by WKDFIRO:<BR><B><BR>As for the passenger side intake, you would have to take out the battery tray. That was done for the Zumalt V8 conversion at Fiero Motorsports...loved that Fiero... </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Post_Engine_1_1.jpg"> <BR>The "Fiero" is still alive and very well here in Michigan. Battery was located to drivers side, air intake trunk routes over into old battery location, and down, with big K&N Cone just behind side scoop. Moving the battery also allow cooling plumbing to come up directly to inlet on engine. Twin throttle body in Lingenfelter 52mm X 2, I could up to the twin 58's but it's very expensive (at 400hp, don't really need anymore). Car was originally Black, no Viper Yellow with new interior.<P>||||reg||6
